On 2007-08-21, Ohle Claussen <ohle.claussen@ds.mpg.de> wrote:
> On 2007-08-21, Derek Ray wrote:
>> (per the changelog, wizards wearing body armor now always have a certain
>> failure percentage to their spells, dependent on level of spell.)
>
> Please enlighten me as to your motives with these wizard changes, here. It

Simply stated:

In vanilla, Wizards can wear as much armor as Barbarians, can have as much
HP as Barbarians, can have nearly identical stats to Barbarians, get
infinitely more Pw than Barbarians, and can cast all the spells at 0%
failure rate... far more than any Barbarian ever could hope for.

Barbarians are considered an 'easy' class.  How easy are Wizards, then?

This doesn't even go into the guaranteed source of infinite one-turn
Elbereth, curse resistance, starting magic resistance, ability to write
any spell they're lacking at 98% success, hideously broken Quest
artifact, etc. etc.

> seems Spork wizards are off best when they use a lot of stacking spells and
> keep them stacked (reflection, protection) to offset armor penalties. Apart

That is certainly the intent.  Whether it actually works out that way
will have to come out in actual play, but people have ascended using the
reflection spell only.  I do need to tack on a 'warning' similar to
protection when the spell is expiring, but other than that it seems to
balance out reasonably well.

> from the rather nice role playing emphasis, is this to offset the near
> infinite mana the Eye offers? Of course, vanilla wizards already make use
> of the Eye by stacking speed and detect monster without noticing much, but
> I'm not sure how easily they could handle protection spells to get into
> decent AC ranges constantly without the 14 points from enchanted DSM.

See above for a more detailed 'why', of course.  The Eye has already
been altered noticeably in Spork to "ramp up" its mana regen based on
the wizard's level, as well as the obvious change of "You have to wear
it now."

It'll be interesting to see how people adapt to protection.  I think
it's a lot more useful for early-game characters than people realize
(though obviously at XL 1 it's not particularly stellar).

> Anyway, quite I like the picture of high level wizards wrapping themselves into
> layers of protective spells instead of armor.

Thanks.  :)  That was sort of where I wanted to go with it, and the
comment in the changelog even implies that to some degree: "So you
wanted to be Mister Spellchucker, huh?"

